{"date": "2026-11-13", "post_id": 1510728, "title": "NGC 206 and the Star Clouds of Andromeda", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-13-ngc-206-and-the-star-clouds-of-andromeda/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> The large stellar association cataloged as <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=990402\">NGC 206</a> is nestled within the dusty arms of the neighboring <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=220119\">Andromeda galaxy</a> along with the galaxy's pinkish star-forming regions. Also known as M31, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/missions/hubble/nasas-hubble-shows-milky-way-is-destined-for-head-on-collision/\">the spiral galaxy is a mere</a> 2.5 million light-years away. NGC 206 is found at the center of <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/astro.midnight/p/DfmV8AJjmn-/\">this sharp and detailed close-up</a> of the southwestern <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=200925\">extent</a> of Andromeda's disk. The bright, blue <a href=\"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2010MNRAS.405.1531E/abstract\">stars of NGC 206</a> indicate its youth. In fact, its youngest massive stars are less than 10 million years old. Much larger than the open or galactic clusters of young stars <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=120912\">in the disk</a><a> of our Milky Way galaxy, </a><a href=\"http://messier.seds.org/more/m031_n206.html\">NGC 206</a> spans about 4,000 light-years. That's comparable in size to the giant stellar nurseries <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=240425\">NGC 604</a> in nearby spiral M33 and the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=240308\">Tarantula Nebula</a> in the Large Magellanic Cloud.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>pixels in space", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/astro.midnight\">David Cruz</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/astro.midnight\">David Cruz</a>", "alt": "Close up of NGC 206 in the Andromeda galaxy.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-13-ngc-206-and-the-star-clouds-of-andromeda/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Andromeda_closeupCruz4096.jpg", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 November 13 - NGC 206 and the Star Clouds of Andromeda</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-13-ngc-206-and-the-star-clouds-of-andromeda/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: The large stellar association cataloged as NGC 206 is nestled within the dusty arms of the neighboring Andromeda galaxy along with the galaxy&#039;s pinkish star-forming regions. Also known as M31, the spiral galaxy is a mere 2.5 million light-years away. NGC 206 is found at the center of this sharp and detailed close-up of the southwestern extent of Andromeda&#039;s disk. The bright, blue stars of NGC 206 indicate its youth. In fact, its youngest massive stars are less than 10 million years old. Much larger than the open or galactic clusters of young stars in the disk of our Milky Way galaxy, NGC 206 spans about 4,000 light-years. That&#039;s comparable in size to the giant stellar nurseries NGC 604 in nearby spiral M33 and the Tarantula Nebula in the Large Magellanic Cloud.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: pixels in space\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"ngc 206 and the star clouds of andromeda\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"NGC 206 and the Star Clouds of Andromeda\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-13-ngc-206-and-the-star-clouds-of-andromeda/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Andromeda_closeupCruz4096.jpg\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Andromeda_closeupCruz4096.jpg\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"Close up of NGC 206 in the Andromeda galaxy.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-11-13T00:05:00-05:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-11-12T12:35:00-05:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"NGC 206 and the Star Clouds of Andromeda\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Andromeda_closeupCruz4096.jpg\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 November 13<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Andromeda_closeupCruz4096.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Andromeda_closeupCruz4096.jpg\" alt=\"Close up of NGC 206 in the Andromeda galaxy.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> NGC 206 and the Star Clouds of Andromeda </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/astro.midnight\">David Cruz</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> The large stellar association cataloged as <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=990402\">NGC 206</a> is nestled within the dusty arms of the neighboring <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=220119\">Andromeda galaxy</a> along with the galaxy's pinkish star-forming regions. Also known as M31, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/missions/hubble/nasas-hubble-shows-milky-way-is-destined-for-head-on-collision/\">the spiral galaxy is a mere</a> 2.5 million light-years away. NGC 206 is found at the center of <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/astro.midnight/p/DfmV8AJjmn-/\">this sharp and detailed close-up</a> of the southwestern <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=200925\">extent</a> of Andromeda's disk. The bright, blue <a href=\"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2010MNRAS.405.1531E/abstract\">stars of NGC 206</a> indicate its youth. In fact, its youngest massive stars are less than 10 million years old. Much larger than the open or galactic clusters of young stars <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=120912\">in the disk</a><a> of our Milky Way galaxy, </a><a href=\"http://messier.seds.org/more/m031_n206.html\">NGC 206</a> spans about 4,000 light-years. That's comparable in size to the giant stellar nurseries <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=240425\">NGC 604</a> in nearby spiral M33 and the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=240308\">Tarantula Nebula</a> in the Large Magellanic Cloud.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>pixels in space\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-12-moon-venus-and-spica-meet/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nJerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Robert Nemiroff, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261113/html"}